.\" Man page of the Router's interface (include/router/).  Render with: man -l router.3
.TH ROUTER 3 2026-09-08 "MITM (omp_reboot)" "MITM Library Functions"
.SH NAME
Router_Push, Router_Pop, Router_Close, Router_Test_drained, Router_Init,
Router_Progress, Router_Test_quiescent, Router_Stats, Router_Reset,
Router_Grab, Router_Release \- route points from sender threads to receiver
threads across MPI ranks
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fB#include "router/router.hpp"\fP
.PP
.BI "Router_thread Router_Init(int " role ", int " group ", MPI_Comm " comm ", int " tag ,
.BI "                          const Router_Opts *" opts );
.fi
.SS Sender
.nf
.BI "void   Router_Push(u64 " a ", u64 " b ", int " dest ", Router_thread &" rt );
.BI "void   Router_Close(Router_thread &" rt );
.fi
.SS Receiver
.nf
.BI "bool   Router_Pop(u64 *" a ", u64 *" b ", Router_thread &" rt );
.BI "bool   Router_Test_drained(Router_thread &" rt );
.BI "size_t Router_Grab(const u64 **" pts ", Router_thread &" rt );
.BI "void   Router_Release(Router_thread &" rt );
.fi
.SS "Service thread (thread 0)"
.nf
.BI "void   Router_Progress(Router_thread &" rt );
.BI "bool   Router_Test_quiescent(const Router_thread &" rt );
.BI "void   Router_Stats(u64 *" stats ", const Router_thread &" rt );
.fi
.SS "Every thread"
.nf
.BI "void   Router_Reset(Router_thread &" rt );
.fi
.SS Queries
Any thread; every one returns
.B int
and takes
.BI "const Router_thread &" rt .
.PP
.nf
Router_rank, Router_local_rank, Router_num_send, Router_num_recv,
Router_local_num_send, Router_local_num_recv, Router_group,
Router_num_groups, Router_group_num_receivers, Router_group_receiver(int \fIi\fP, \fIrt\fP),
Router_domain, Router_num_domains, Router_cpu, Router_numa_node
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The Router carries
.IR points ,
pairs of u64, from
.I sender
threads to globally numbered
.I receiver
threads across the ranks of an MPI communicator.
It runs one rank per node, a node's threads being one OpenMP team.
Every thread of the team holds one handle, its own
.BR Router_thread ,
returned by
.BR Router_Init ()
and passed to every later call; a thread never calls with another's handle.
.PP
Thread 0 of the team is the
.IR "service thread" :
it does all the MPI and moves the points, and neither sends nor receives
points itself.
Every other thread is a sender or a receiver, as it declares to
.BR Router_Init ().
.PP
No point is ever lost: a sender may block until the Router has room, and every
point pushed is delivered exactly once.
.PP
Time is cut into
.IR rounds .
Within a round the senders push, then close; the receivers read until drained;
the service thread progresses until quiescent.
The round ends with
.BR Router_Reset (),
collective over the team (see
.BR "THE ROUND" ).
.SS Router_Push()
Hands the point
.RI ( a ", " b )
to the Router for delivery to receiver
.IR dest ,
0 <=
.I dest
< Router_num_recv(\fIrt\fP): the receiver's
.BR Router_rank ().
Delivery is batched: a pushed point may reach its receiver much later, and
possibly not before the sender closes.
The call may block until the Router has room, and the point is delivered
exactly once.
.SS Router_Pop()
Takes one point delivered to this receiver: stores it in
.RI * a ", *" b
and returns true, or returns false when no point is available now.
Never waits.
A false means "try again", not "nothing more will come": the receiver loops on
.BR Router_Pop ()
until
.BR Router_Test_drained ()
returns true.
.SS Router_Close()
Declares that this sender pushes nothing more in this round.
A closed sender must not push again before
.BR Router_Reset ().
The round cannot end until every sender of every node has closed.
.SS Router_Test_drained()
Returns true once nothing more can be delivered to this receiver in this round
and it holds nothing: nothing delivered and unread, no block out (see
.BR "ADVANCED API" ).
.SS Router_Init()
Collective over every thread of the team on every node, called once per thread
inside one OpenMP parallel region.
.IR comm ", " tag " and " opts
are the same on every thread of every node.
.TP
.I role
.B ROUTER_SENDER
or
.BR ROUTER_RECEIVER .
Thread 0 becomes the service thread whatever it passes.
.TP
.I group
.BR ROUTER_GROUP_AUTO ,
or the group this thread belongs to, in 0..G\-1 (see
.BR PLACEMENT ).
.TP
.I comm
The MPI communicator, used as is; one rank per node.
.TP
.I tag
The tag of every Router message.
The caller sends nothing else on
.I comm
with this tag.
.TP
.I opts
The options (see
.BR OPTIONS ),
or
.BR ROUTER_DEFAULT_OPTS .
.PP
It returns once the whole team on every node is connected: the shape is agreed
with the peers, every thread is pinned, every handle exists.
A node needs at least one sender and one receiver; every node must have the
same number of each.
.PP
The handle is the caller's, kept named for the whole life of the team; it is
neither copyable nor assignable.
Destroying the handles while a round is in progress aborts.
.SS Router_Progress(), Router_Test_quiescent()
Service thread only.
.BR Router_Progress ()
does one bounded, non-blocking turn of the service thread's work: it moves
points from the senders to the network and to the receivers, and brings the
round to its end once every sender has closed.
The service thread calls it in a loop; nothing moves between two calls.
It does nothing once the node is quiescent.
.BR Router_Test_quiescent ()
returns true when this node has sent and received everything of the round and
its receivers hold nothing.
.SS Router_Stats()
Service thread only.
Fills
.IR stats ,
an array of
.B ROUTER_STATS_SIZE
u64 indexed by
.BR "enum router_stat" ,
with the node's tallies for the round, all threads summed (see
.BR STATISTICS ).
Exact once quiescent, a snapshot before.
Read it before
.BR Router_Reset (),
which clears it.
.SS Router_Reset()
Collective over every thread of the team on every node.
Back to a fresh round: once every thread of the node has entered the call, the
tallies and the round's state are cleared, and an
.B MPI_Barrier
on
.I comm
guarantees that no node starts the next round before every node has left this
one.
The call returns, on every thread, only after that: nothing is pushed or popped
before the Router is fresh everywhere.
The service thread must be quiescent and a receiver must hold no block (see
.BR "ADVANCED API" ).
.SH QUERIES
Any thread, once
.BR Router_Init ()
has returned.
A query marked (worker) aborts on the service thread.
.TP
.BI Router_local_rank( rt )
(worker) The caller's index among the threads of its role on this node, from 0.
.TP
.BI Router_rank( rt )
(worker) Its index among its role on all nodes: the nodes in rank order, each
node's threads by local rank.
A receiver's is the
.I dest
that reaches it.
.TP
.BI Router_local_num_send( rt )
Senders on this node.
.TP
.BI Router_local_num_recv( rt )
Receivers on this node.
.TP
.BI Router_num_send( rt )
Senders over all nodes.
.TP
.BI Router_num_recv( rt )
Receivers over all nodes.
.TP
.BI Router_group( rt )
The caller's group, 0..G\-1; \-1 for the service thread.
.TP
.BI Router_num_groups( rt )
G, the groups of this node.
.TP
.BI Router_group_num_receivers( rt )
(worker) The receivers in the caller's group.
.TP
.BI Router_group_receiver( i ", " rt )
(worker) The local rank of the
.IR i -th
receiver of the caller's group.
.TP
.BI Router_domain( rt )
The cache domain the caller was pinned in; \-1 when not pinned.
.TP
.BI Router_num_domains( rt )
The node's cache domains; 0 when not pinned.
.TP
.BI Router_cpu( rt )
The CPU the kernel reported for the caller after pinning; momentary when not
pinned.
.TP
.BI Router_numa_node( rt )
Its NUMA node, likewise.
.SH PLACEMENT
By default the Router decides where every thread of the team runs and which
threads work together.
The caller sees the outcome through the queries above.
.SS The CPU set
The node's CPU set is the union of the affinity masks the threads inherit from
the launcher and the OpenMP runtime.
When
.I opts->pin
is true the Router must own that set: it refuses a set with fewer CPUs than the
team has threads, and a set that overlaps another rank's on the same host.
Run with
.BR "\-\-bind\-to none" ,
give each rank disjoint CPUs
.RB ( "\-\-map\-by numa \-\-bind\-to numa" ", " "\-\-map\-by slot:PE=n" ),
or set
.I pin
= false.
.SS Pinning
The set's cores are partitioned into
.IR "cache domains" :
the cores sharing one cache at the lowest level shared by several cores (the L3
on Xeon and EPYC alike), or at the level
.I opts->cache_level
names.
If the CPUs share no such cache, the whole set is one domain.
Then:
.IP 1. 4
The service thread takes a core of domain 0, alone.
.IP 2. 4
The receivers, then the senders, are dealt round-robin over the domains, one
cursor for the whole team starting after the service thread's domain.
Each takes the emptiest core of its domain, the one with the fewest threads
placed so far; when every core of the domain is taken, the emptiest core of the
whole set.
Every thread thus has a core of its own while cores last, and SMT siblings fill
up only after that.
.IP 3. 4
Every thread pins itself to its CPU and asks the kernel where it runs.
If any thread is not where the plan put it,
.BR Router_Init ()
aborts the run
.RB ( MPI_Abort )
before any handle is built.
.PP
Afterwards
.BR Router_cpu (),
.BR Router_numa_node ()
and
.BR Router_domain ()
report the measured placement of the calling thread.
.SS Groups
A
.I group
is a set of senders and receivers that work together: a consumer pairs each
sender with a receiver of its group,
.BR Router_group_receiver ().
There are G groups per node, numbered 0..G\-1; every worker belongs to one, the
service thread to none.
.PP
.B "Automatic groups."
When every worker passes
.BR ROUTER_GROUP_AUTO ,
each cache domain is cut into the fewest groups of at most
.I opts->group_size
cores, and every receiver, then every sender, joins the group of its domain
with the fewest members of its role so far.
A group therefore sits inside one cache domain, both roles spread evenly over
its domain's groups.
.PP
.B "Caller's groups."
When a worker passes a group number, every worker must: the numbers are 0..G\-1
with no gap and no empty group, as for
.BR MPI_Comm_split ().
The groups are then the caller's, the pinning still the Router's.
.SS Without pinning
With
.I opts->pin
= false the threads stay where the caller left them and the CPU set is not
checked.
.BR Router_domain ()
returns \-1,
.BR Router_num_domains ()
0.
Automatic groups fall out of the worker index: G = max(ceil(S / group_size),
ceil(R / group_size), 1), the receivers and the senders each dealt over them
round-robin.
Caller's groups apply as above.
.SH ADVANCED API
Points are delivered in
.I blocks
of
.I opts->block_points
points.
A receiver may read a block where it lies instead of popping its points one by
one.
.SS Router_Grab(), Router_Release()
.BR Router_Grab ()
returns the number of points in the next block delivered to this receiver and
sets
.RI * pts
to its memory: point k is
.RI ( pts "[2k], " pts [2k+1]).
It returns 0, with
.RI * pts
= NULL, when no block is available now, and never waits.
One block is out at a time:
.BR Router_Release ()
gives it back to the Router, after which none of its memory may be read.
A second
.BR Router_Grab ()
before the release, or a
.BR Router_Release ()
with no block out, aborts.
.PP
A receiver uses either
.BR Router_Grab ()/ Router_Release ()
or
.BR Router_Pop (),
not both.
.SH OPTIONS
.BR "struct Router_Opts" .
Every field has a working default and every node must supply the same values:
the nodes compare them at
.BR Router_Init (),
and on a disagreement the defaults are used everywhere, with a warning.
Pass
.B ROUTER_DEFAULT_OPTS
for the defaults.
.TP
.BI "size_t " block_points " = 4096"
Points per block, one block being one message.
A power of two >= 4.
.TP
.BI "size_t " swc_linesize " = 0"
Points a sender accumulates for one destination before they leave its hands.
0 chooses from the fan-out; else a power of two in [4, block_points], with
block_points / swc_linesize <= 4096.
.TP
.BI "int    " n_recv " = 32"
MPI receives kept posted, and as many sends in flight at most.
>= 1.
.TP
.BI "int    " inbox_blocks " = 64"
Blocks that may wait for one receiver at once.
>= 1.
.TP
.BI "int    " sweep_blocks " = 256"
Blocks the service thread ships per
.BR Router_Progress ()
at most: the bound on one turn.
>= 1.
.TP
.BI "int    " credit " = 4"
Blocks in flight to one peer node at most.
>= 1.
.TP
.BI "int    " dests_per_node " = 0"
For the benchmark only; leave at 0.
.TP
.BI "bool   " pin " = true"
The Router pins the threads and forms the groups (see
.BR PLACEMENT ).
.TP
.BI "int    " cache_level " = 0"
The cache level a group is confined to; 0 detects the lowest level shared by
several cores.
>= 0.
.TP
.BI "int    " group_size " = 16"
Cores per group at most: a larger cache domain is cut into several groups.
>= 1.
.TP
.BI "bool   " verbose " = true"
Rank 0 prints the sizes, the planned layout and the measured layout at
.BR Router_Init ().
.SH STATISTICS
.B enum router_stat
indexes the array
.BR Router_Stats ()
fills.
.TP
.B ROUTER_PUSHED, ROUTER_POPPED
Points pushed by this node's senders; points delivered to its receivers.
.TP
.B ROUTER_SENT, ROUTER_RECV, ROUTER_LOCAL
Points sent to and received from other nodes; points delivered to a receiver of
this node without leaving it.
.TP
.B ROUTER_MSGS_SENT, ROUTER_MSGS_RECV, ROUTER_BYTES_SENT, ROUTER_BYTES_RECV
MPI messages and bytes, each way.
.TP
.B ROUTER_BLOCKS
Blocks recycled, by the receivers and by the service thread.
.TP
.B ROUTER_STALL_OUT
Blocks the service thread had to hold back because their destination had no
room yet.
.TP
.B ROUTER_STALL_IN
Receives the service thread could not keep posted for want of a free block.
.TP
.B ROUTER_TURNS, ROUTER_IDLE_TURNS
Calls to
.BR Router_Progress ();
of which those that moved nothing.
.SH THE ROUND
.IP 1. 4
Senders call
.BR Router_Push ()
until they have nothing more, then
.BR Router_Close ().
.IP 2. 4
Receivers call
.BR Router_Pop ()
(or
.BR Router_Grab ()/ Router_Release ())
until
.BR Router_Test_drained ().
.IP 3. 4
The service thread calls
.BR Router_Progress ()
until
.BR Router_Test_quiescent (),
then reads
.BR Router_Stats ().
.IP 4. 4
Every thread calls
.BR Router_Reset ().
The next round may begin.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
#pragma omp parallel
{
    int tid  = omp_get_thread_num();
    int role = (tid <= R) ? ROUTER_RECEIVER : ROUTER_SENDER;   /* thread 0's is ignored */
    Router_thread rt = Router_Init(role, ROUTER_GROUP_AUTO, MPI_COMM_WORLD, 42,
                                   ROUTER_DEFAULT_OPTS);
    int n_recv = Router_num_recv(rt);
    for (int round = 0; round < N; round++) {
        if (tid == 0) {
            while (!Router_Test_quiescent(rt))
                Router_Progress(rt);
        } else if (role == ROUTER_SENDER) {
            for (u64 i = 0; i < per_sender; i++)
                Router_Push(key(i), val(i), key(i) % n_recv, rt);
            Router_Close(rt);
        } else {
            u64 a, b;
            while (!Router_Test_drained(rt))
                if (Router_Pop(&a, &b, rt))
                    consume(a, b);
        }
        if (tid == 0) {
            u64 st[ROUTER_STATS_SIZE];
            Router_Stats(st, rt);
        }
        Router_Reset(rt);
    }
}   /* every handle dies here; the router must be quiescent */
.EE
.SH ERRORS
No function returns an error code.
An invalid argument, a call on a thread of the wrong role, a broken
precondition (a second grab, a release with nothing out, a reset before
quiescence or with a block out) or an MPI library initialised below
.B MPI_THREAD_FUNNELED
aborts the process through
.BR errx (3).
A thread the kernel did not pin where planned aborts the run through
.BR MPI_Abort ().
.SH NOTES
Two ranks on one host cannot both pin (see
.BR PLACEMENT ):
run them with
.I pin
= false, or give each its own CPUs.
.PP
The Router sends nothing on
.I comm
outside a round and the barrier of
.BR Router_Reset ().
